review_comments.body:
I feel like we may have some circular or redundant logic going on.  Lemme rubber duck this and see if I can suss it out.

Previously this was checking `node.peer` but now it's checking `peer`.  But earlier on line 55 if `peer` is truthy we are also setting `to.peer` to `true`.  Then we're unsetting it if we don't have an incoming peer edge.

This means the only use case for which we are clearing `to.peer` is if it was set to `true` by something other than us.

So: if this is a peer edge, mark the target as peer.  If this is not a peer edge, clear the peer flag if we do not have an incoming peer edge.

Ok I see where the inefficiency is.  We do not need to iterate through the incoming edges if `to.peer` is not set.

```javascript
if (peer) {
  to.peer = true
} else if (to.peer && !hasIncomingPeerEdge(to)) {
  unsetFlag(to, 'peer')
}
```

This can replace lines 54-58 and 69-73.

